# Overview
#
# A fancy word stands in for a plainer one (`delve into` for `read`,
# `showcase` for `show`, `tapestry` for `mix`). The fancy word adds
# length and no meaning, and a reader who does not know it must look it
# up. Each word or phrase here turns up far more in generated text than
# in docs a person wrote. The fix writes the plain word. The rule sits
# at Recommended, because the plain word differs from word to word, and
# a name can hold one of the words.
#
# Expression
#
# `$foster`, `$showcase`, `$garner`, and `$boast` expand to every form
# of the word through Harper's dictionary. A space in a pattern is a
# whitespace token, so `deep[-, ( )]dive` lists a hyphen and a space.
# The pattern matches the spaced and the hyphenated spelling alike.
#
# `(landscape of)` does not open with `the`, so the branch also matches
# `the changing landscape of` and `a landscape of tools`.
#
# The two `elevate` branches name each form of the verb, because
# `$elevate` also expands to the noun `elevation`. `privilege` lists the
# words for a privilege. `ahead` matches a word from `privilege`, alone
# or after one, two, or three other words (`their Kubernetes RBAC
# privileges`). A list picks its longest match and never backtracks, so
# each gap length is its own alternative. `gap` matches one of the other
# words. In a pattern, `*` matches one token. A hyphen is a token of its
# own, and so is the apostrophe that ends a plural possessive. `gap`
# therefore lists `*-*` beside `*` for a word with one hyphen (`its
# cluster-admin privileges`) and `*'` for a plural possessive (`the
# users' privileges`).
#
# Each branch skips a verb when `ahead` matches the words after it. A
# fancy `elevate` therefore passes near a privilege word (`the redesign
# elevates the permissions page`). The negated `ahead` after a space
# needs a word to consume, so a verb before a comma or at the end of a
# sentence passes.
#
# `elevated` counts only before `to` or before a word that opens an
# object (`was elevated to GA status`, `elevated the discussion`).
# Harper's part-of-speech tagger marks such a word `DET` or `PRON`.
# `DET` is a determiner, a word that introduces a noun, such as `the`,
# `a`, or `this`, and `PRON` is a pronoun. The filter applies the
# `ahead` guard and then narrows the next word to one of the three. The
# verb `elevated` therefore passes before a noun that has no determiner
# (`the talk elevated developer experience`).
#
# False Positives
#
# `unlock`, `harness`, `landscape`, `realm`, and `underscore` are left
# out, because each names something in code (a mutex, a test harness, a
# page orientation, a Kerberos realm, the character). The stock phrases
# built on them count instead (`harness the power`, `unlock the full
# potential`, `landscape of`, `the realm of`).
#
# `elevate privileges` names a security operation, so a verb with a
# privilege word after it passes (`elevate their privileges`, `elevates
# permission for the install`). The security sense still matches when
# no privilege word follows the verb (`the helper elevates through
# sudo`, `the process was elevated to administrator`). `gap` matches
# only a plain word, a word with one hyphen, or a plural possessive. The
# verb therefore still matches before a privilege word when the gap
# holds other punctuation, such as a slash, quotes, a comma, or a second
# hyphen (`elevate read/write permissions`, `elevate read, write, and
# admin permissions`). The verb also still matches when four or more
# words sit between it and the privilege word (`elevate their own
# cluster-wide RBAC privileges`).
#
# `elevated` is also an adjective for code that runs with administrator
# rights (`an elevated prompt`), so `elevated` before a noun passes. The
# adjective after a verb still matches before `to`, a determiner, or a
# pronoun (`run the terminal elevated to edit the hosts file`, `the
# installer runs elevated the whole time`, `admins running elevated who
# forget to drop rights`).
#
# A name that holds one of the words also matches (`Learning Journey
# Working Group`).
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Required ProseFancyWord holds `utilize` and `leverage`, whose
# plain word is always `use`. The Recommended ProseFancyWord holds the
# words that turn up far more in generated text than in docs a person
# wrote. Their plain word differs from word to word. The Optional
# ProseFancyWord holds the words that human-written docs also use.
#
# ProseIdiom holds `under the hood`, `at its core`, and `strike a
# balance`, whose fix writes the literal meaning. ProseImportance holds
# `underscores the importance`.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 60 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 20 of 20 sampled
# hits stood in for a plainer word. Five lines in the corpus hold a form
# of `elevate`. Four name the security operation and pass. The rule
# reports the fifth (`was elevated to GA status`), where `promoted` is
# the plain word.
expr privilege [privilege, privileges, permission, permissions]
expr gap [*, (*-*), (*')]
expr ahead [@privilege, (@gap @privilege), (@gap @gap @privilege), (@gap @gap @gap @privilege)]
expr main [
    delve,
    delves,
    delving,
    (dive into),
    (deep[-, ( )]dive),
    unleash,
    unleashes,
    empower,
    empowers,
    empowering,
    streamline,
    streamlines,
    streamlined,
    ([elevate, elevates, elevating] !@ahead),
    (elevated <!@ahead, [DET, PRON, to]>),
    tapestry,
    journey,
    ([navigate, navigates, navigating] the [complexities, complexity, landscape, world, challenges]),
    testament,
    $foster,
    $showcase,
    $garner,
    $boast,
    interplay,
    intricacies,
    synergy,
    nuanced,
    multifaceted,
    meticulous,
    meticulously,
    bolster,
    bolsters,
    bolstered,
    bolstering,
    spearhead,
    spearheads,
    spearheaded,
    spearheading,
    supercharge,
    supercharges,
    supercharged,
    supercharging,
    revolutionize,
    revolutionizes,
    revolutionized,
    revolutionizing,
    embark,
    embarks,
    embarked,
    embarking,
    ([harness, harnesses, harnessing] the power),
    ([unlock, unlocks, unlocking] the potential),
    ([unlock, unlocks, unlocking] the [full, true] potential),
    (the realm of),
    (landscape of),
    (key takeaway),
    (key takeaways)
]
let message "Fancy word. Use the plain word it stands for."
let description "Flags fancy words and phrases, such as `delve`, `showcase`, and `tapestry`, that stand in for a plainer word."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "Unlock releases the mutex."
allows "The test harness builds the fixture once."
allows "The realm names the Kerberos domain."
allows "Names with an underscore are private."
allows "The installer elevates privileges with sudo."
allows "The script elevates privilege for the install."
allows "The script elevates permission for the install."
allows "An attacker can further elevate their privileges."
allows "The wrapper elevates its own permissions."
allows "The attack works without elevating their Kubernetes RBAC privileges."
allows "The bug lets a pod elevate its cluster-admin privileges."
allows "The attacker elevates root-level permissions."
allows "The bug lets a pod elevate the users' privileges."
allows "The service runs with elevated privileges."
allows "The exploit elevated the privileges of the process."
allows "Run the installer from an elevated prompt."
allows "Rotate the page to landscape orientation before printing."
